On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 10:53:12 -0700, Pat Maddox wrote:

> I want to take a step back. No new patches this round. Just rethinking
> the design.

Understood. No patches with this reply. The short answer: Dovecot and
OpenSMTPD each earn their place, and LMTP is the one we could drop.
OpenSMTPD's maildir delivery would replace it at little cost.

> Basically I want to know what value each component brings.

Dovecot is the store and the IMAP server. It is the only part that
enforces the rights: any login may read, only pat@p7x.dev and mbsync
may change anything. Every host syncs from it, and mbsync syncs it
with Fastmail. Nothing else in the design can do that, so it stays
whatever we decide below.

OpenSMTPD is the front door. It is where the posting rules live, in
the SMTP session, while the client is still connected:

- It sorts each RCPT TO. The list's addresses are delivered, the known
  addresses (pat@p7x.dev, pat@patmaddox.com, claude@p7x.dev) are
  accepted and discarded, and anything else gets a 5xx. That is what
  makes a reply to all land once, with To and Cc intact, and makes a
  typo fail at the client instead of vanishing.
- It refuses a header it cannot parse before it answers DATA, and
  adds a missing Message-ID and Date.
- It speaks plain SMTP on 2525, which is what msmtp, git send-email,
  and any mail client speak by default.
- It logs each session in smtpd.log, so a refused post can be traced.

mbsync is the only part that talks to Fastmail and the only holder of
the Fastmail credentials.

LMTP is not a component of its own. It is how OpenSMTPD hands a
message to Dovecot. All it buys us is the rule that Dovecot is the
only program that writes the maildir.

> Do we need OpenSMTPD here? If LMTP can deliver messages directly - can
> we call it directly, and take OpenSMTPD out of the mix?

We could. msmtp supports "protocol lmtp", and Dovecot can listen for
LMTP on 127.0.0.1 as well as on its socket. But what we would lose is
the posting rules above, and putting them back costs more than
OpenSMTPD does:

- Dovecot's LMTP delivers to whatever user its userdb names, and ours
  accepts every user. To refuse unknown addresses we would need a
  userdb that lists each address. To discard the known addresses we
  would need Sieve (mail/dovecot-pigeonhole), a new dependency, and
  you said Sieve is outside this spec.
- Dovecot does not refuse a malformed header. It stores whatever it
  is given. msmtp adds a missing Date and Message-ID, but other
  clients may not.
- git send-email speaks SMTP only, so it would have to go through
  msmtp. It does today, but that becomes a requirement rather than a
  choice.
- LMTP is meant for an MTA talking to the store it trusts. Opening it
  on loopback lets every jail on beastie talk straight to the store
  with no policy in between.

We would also lose the queue, but that one does not matter much.
With LMTP called directly, a post while Dovecot is down fails at the
client, which arguably beats a 250 followed by a delayed delivery.

> Or should we treat LMTP as basically an optimization, but the initial
> SMTP server should be full featured.

That is close to the current design, with one change of words. The
SMTP server is where all the policy lives, and LMTP is only the last
hop into the store. "Full featured" should mean the policy above,
though, not a full MTA. Relaying, outside mail, auth, and TLS are all
out of scope, which is why the spec chose OpenSMTPD over Postfix.

> Also doesn't OpenSMTPD have local delivery? Why can't it just deliver
> straight to maildir?

It can, and having looked again, the reason we gave does not hold for
it. smtpd.conf would say

    action "list" maildir "/data/mail/maildir/INBOX" virtual <to-vmail>

The spec's case for "Dovecot as the only writer" is about three
writers running as three users. mbsync creates files 0600 and folders
0700, renames and unlinks need write on each folder, and each writer
keeps its own record of the files. That holds for mbsync, and keeping
mbsync on IMAP is still right. It does not hold for OpenSMTPD:

- Its delivery already runs as vmail, Dovecot's own user, so there is
  no permission problem.
- It only ever adds a file, through tmp/ into INBOX/new/, which is the
  case maildir was designed for. It never renames or removes.
- Dovecot treats a new file in new/ as new mail. It picks it up on
  its next look at the folder and gives it a UID.

What we would gain:

- One fewer protocol. Dovecot drops "lmtp" from protocols, the LMTP
  service and its socket go, and lmtp_add_received_header goes too.
- A post no longer depends on Dovecot running. Step 11 of the
  acceptance test, a post while Dovecot is stopped, becomes true at
  once instead of after a retry from the queue.

What we would give up, all small:

- The rule shrinks from "only Dovecot writes the list" to "only
  Dovecot changes or removes a message; OpenSMTPD only adds new ones
  to INBOX".
- Return-Path and Delivered-To would come from OpenSMTPD instead of
  Dovecot. I expect it to write both, but I have not checked that
  Delivered-To names the list address with the virtual mapping. That
  has to be verified on a jail.
- An IDLE client may see a new post a little later than it would with
  LMTP, since Dovecot learns of it from the filesystem rather than at
  delivery. Polling clients and the one-minute sync see no difference.

My recommendation is to keep OpenSMTPD and switch its delivery from
LMTP to maildir. It is a small change on top of v10. "[PATCH v10 07/10]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Take posts with OpenSMTPD" [1] loses its
LMTP half, and the spec's "only writer" paragraph gets rewritten. If
you agree, I will send it as v11 with the spec change in the same
series.

